{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.SpechtActionInjective","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.SpechtActionInjective","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.SpechtRegularMultiplicity","lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.OfModuleAsModule","lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.MaschkeSnSemisimple","lean:module:Mathlib.RingTheory.SimpleModule.Basic"],"declarations":6,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.SpechtActionInjective.factorial_le_sum_sq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.SpechtActionInjective.factorial_le_sum_sq","kind":"theorem","line":162,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/SpechtActionInjective.lean#L162-L178","doc":"**`n! ≤ Σ_{μ ⊢ n} (dim_k S^μ)²`.**\n\nInjectivity of the Specht action map, read through the two dimension computations of\n`SpechtBlockDimension`.\n\n**This is one inequality, not the RSK identity.** The reverse `Σ_μ (dim S^μ)² ≤ n!` needs the\nblocks not to overlap — the isotypic independence of the multiplicity spaces — and is not\nproved here. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.SpechtActionInjective.spechtProduct_injective","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.SpechtActionInjective.spechtProduct_injective","kind":"theorem","line":111,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/SpechtActionInjective.lean#L111-L158","doc":"**The Specht action map is injective.**\n\nAn `x` in the kernel kills every Specht module; the classification\n(`specht_exhausts_irreducibles`) makes every simple `k[S_n]`-module a Specht module, so `x`\nkills every simple; semisimplicity (Maschke) then makes it kill `k[S_n]` itself, and\n`x = x • 1 = 0`.\n\n`-- Ref: [sagan2001] §1.10; [james1984representation]`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.SpechtActionInjective.spechtProduct_apply","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.SpechtActionInjective.spechtProduct_apply","kind":"theorem","line":107,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/SpechtActionInjective.lean#L107-L109","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.SpechtActionInjective.spechtProduct","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.SpechtActionInjective.spechtProduct","kind":"def","line":100,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/SpechtActionInjective.lean#L100-L104","doc":"The product of the Specht actions, `Φ : k[S_n] →ₐ[k] ∏_{μ ⊢ n} End_k(S^μ)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.SpechtActionInjective.asAlgebraHom_of_intertwiner","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.SpechtActionInjective.asAlgebraHom_of_intertwiner","kind":"theorem","line":78,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/SpechtActionInjective.lean#L78-L94","doc":"**Transport of the algebra action along a rep-iso.** A `k`-linear equivalence intertwining\ntwo representations intertwines the induced `MonoidAlgebra` actions.\n\nThe hypothesis is stated on group elements; extending it to a general `x : k[G]` is a\n`Finsupp` induction, using `Representation.asAlgebraHom_single`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.SpechtActionInjective.eq_zero_of_smul_simples_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.SpechtActionInjective.eq_zero_of_smul_simples_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":59,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/SpechtActionInjective.lean#L59-L76","doc":"**The semisimple sweep.** In a semisimple module, an element of the ring that kills every\nsimple submodule kills everything.\n\nThe supremum of a family of submodules consists of finite sums of their elements\n(`Submodule.iSup_induction` — note it has no `smul` case, which is what makes this work:\n`y ↦ x • y = 0` is closed under addition but *not* under multiplication by the ring on the\nright, since the ring need not be commutative). ","signatureChunk":0}]}